WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:10.720
Some YouTube creators are upset that YouTube is automatically demonetizing certain video game videos

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:12.100
with its machine-learning algorithm.

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:16.860
The suggested explanation is the depiction of guns, though YouTube denies this.

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:20.820
Gamers who previously made money on the platform
now find their revenue drying up.

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:24.200
In the big picture, the issue is about how
to fund content and its delivery.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:29.600
Now, gamers or game companies could make their own platform and charge a fee for users to participate,

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:33.780
but many prefer advertising-funded content rather than paying out-of-pocket.

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:37.280
The trouble is that advertising dollars are increasingly concentrated with

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:38.609
a few ad platforms.

00:00:38.609 --> 00:00:44.600
Unfortunately, this trend has been strengthened in part by the FCC's Title II net neutrality rules, which block

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:49.840
game makers and gamers from finding alternative models for advertising, particularly from their ISP.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:53.820
In the meantime, we should welcome the emergence
of alternative platforms Patreon and

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:59.949
Twitch, but we also need more diversity in the business
models to bring competition to the concentrated

00:00:59.949 --> 00:01:00.949
ad platform market.

00:01:00.949 --> 00:01:04.880
Removing the FCC’s net neutrality
rules is a step in the right direction.

